target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestDroppedGatesCollapsesTheFalsyStatusesAStringCanHold'
expect: gate g_empty reported
why:    RESTORED, narrowed, then RE-ANCHORED for GateStatus. This entry was retired as "now unreachable", which was wrong: typing turned `false`/`0`/`null` into decode errors, but the EMPTY STRING is still a status a string can hold, and defaulting only on ABSENCE reports "" as the gate's status — an empty status names nothing about the gate, and the status is the whole content of the alarm line. The Python's `st or "unreported"` collapses all of them. The collapse now reads `res.Status.Value != ""`, which covers absent, null and "" in one test because GateStatus's zero value is the empty string; mutating it to the presence flag restores the three-way split at the one reader that must not have it.
--- old
			if res, ok := r.Finish.Executed[name]; ok && res.Status.Value != "" {
--- new
			if res, ok := r.Finish.Executed[name]; ok && res.Status.Present {
